# {{.Name}}

A [Ryla](https://github.com/Dshonored/ryla) application, on MongoDB.

## Getting started

Point `MONGO_URI` in `.env` at a server, then:

```bash
ry migrate   # create the indexes the application declares
ry dev       # start the dev server with live reload
```

The app listens on http://localhost:8080.

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `ry dev` | Watch, rebuild, restart, and reload the browser |
| `ry build` | Compile a single static binary |
| `ry start` | Run the compiled binary |
| `ry migrate` | Create every declared index |
| `ry migrate:status` | List the indexes on the server, declared or not |
| `ry make:controller Posts` | Generate a controller |
| `ry make:job SendEmail` | Generate a background job |
| `ry queue:work` | Run background jobs |
| `ry schedule:run` | Run recurring tasks |
| `ry make:request CreatePost` | Generate a validated request type |
| `ry key:generate` | Generate the key that signs cookies |
| `ry make:model Post` | Generate a document type and its index declaration |
| `ry routes` | List named routes |
| `ry make:test Posts` | Generate a feature test |

## No migrations

A document store has no schema to migrate. Adding a field means writing
documents that have it, and older documents simply do not — so there is nothing
for a migration to alter and nothing for a rollback to undo.

Indexes are the part that genuinely has to be declared, created before anything
relies on them, and kept in step across environments. Each model declares its
own from `init`; `ry migrate` applies them. Creating an index that already
exists is a no-op, so it is safe on every deploy.

`ry migrate:status` lists what the server actually has, marking any index that
is not declared in the code — usually one created by hand, or left over from a
deploy that has since dropped the declaration.

## Authentication

`ry make:auth` and `ry make:2fa` are written against GORM, so they are not
available on MongoDB and will tell you so rather than write files that cannot
compile. The framework packages behind them still work: `auth` for password
hashing and verification, `session` for the session store — already wired, and
already backed by MongoDB — and `twofactor` for TOTP secrets and recovery codes.
Personal access tokens (`bearer`) are GORM-only and would need a collection of
your own.

[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) lists the decisions the SQL scaffold makes for you, so
that hand-writing this does not quietly drop one.

## Tests

```bash
MONGO_TEST_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017 go test ./...
```

`tests/example_test.go` builds the whole application and makes requests through
the real router and middleware stack:

```go
client(t).Get("/").AssertOK().AssertContains("Welcome")
```

They need a server, so they skip unless `MONGO_TEST_URI` names one — and they
drop the database they use, so point it somewhere disposable. The client keeps
cookies between requests, carries the CSRF token on every form submission, and
`Login(user.ID.Hex())` signs it in without going through the login form. Copy
the file for your own tests, or generate one with `ry make:test Posts`.

## The welcome page

The page at `/` is a demo: it exercises a route, a controller, a JSON endpoint
and a compiled view, and tells you which files to edit. Delete it when you have
your own — `app/controllers/demo.go`, the `/demo/counter` routes, and
`resources/static/demo.js`.

`resources/static/app.css` is a design system rather than demo styling: tokens
at the top, then base elements, then components. Change the tokens and the whole
application restyles.

## Layout

```
app/            controllers, models, middleware — your code
app/models/     document types and the indexes they declare
app/requests/   what a form may contain, and what counts as valid
config/         typed configuration, read from the environment
database/       connection defaults and seeders
resources/      templ views and static assets
routes/         the route table
tests/          feature tests through the real router
storage/        logs and build scratch
cmd/app/        the binary's entrypoint
```

## Working with coding agents

[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) describes the layout, the conventions and which files are
generated, so Claude Code, Cursor and similar tools do not hand-edit `*_templ.go`
or rewrite `routes/web.go`. `CLAUDE.md` points at the same file.

## Deploying

`ry build` produces one static binary with the views and static assets compiled
in. Copy it and the `.env` file to the server; nothing else is required — no Go
toolchain and no `ry`. A MongoDB server is, of course, still required.

```bash
./{{.Name}} migrate
./{{.Name}} serve
```
